  <h2>The Problem</h2>
  <p>
    Cognitive function is not a fixed trait — it is a metric you can train, protect, or
    neglect. Memory lapses, focus problems, word-finding gaps, and mental fatigue are not
    inevitable features of aging. They are feedback. When caught early, they are reversible.
    When ignored, they compound into something much harder to address.
  </p>
  <p>
    The brain follows a ruthless use-it-or-lose-it principle. Passive screen consumption,
    fragmented attention, chronic sleep debt, and untreated metabolic dysfunction collectively
    degrade the neural architecture that supports sharp thinking. Most men don't notice the
    slope until the decline is significant.
  </p>
  <p>
    Mind is the destination of the entire 4M framework. Everything else — gut health, hormones,
    sleep, metabolic health — feeds into cognitive output. The Mind pillar is where you track,
    train, and protect the thing all of this is for.
  </p>

  <h2>The Insulting Behaviors</h2>
  <p>These are the most common ways men actively accelerate their own cognitive decline.</p>
  <ul class="insult-list">
    <li>Chronic poor sleep — the glymphatic system clears amyloid and metabolic waste during deep sleep only</li>
    <li>Sedentary lifestyle — movement drives BDNF (brain-derived neurotrophic factor), the most important brain-protective molecule</li>
    <li>No cognitive training — the brain adapts to the demands placed on it, and passive consumption makes no demands</li>
    <li>Excess alcohol — even moderate chronic use accelerates hippocampal atrophy</li>
    <li>Untreated gut inflammation — the gut-brain axis directly modulates neuroinflammation</li>
    <li>Untreated hormonal decline — testosterone and thyroid are critical substrates for cognitive function</li>
    <li>Scrolling-style information consumption — breaks the focus muscle through constant context-switching</li>
  </ul>

